WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.412
Testing should exercise temporary file behavior directly.

2
00:00:03.412 --> 00:00:13.247
Unit tests can verify that vetted APIs are used, names are not manually
predictable, paths remain inside approved directories, and cleanup happens
after normal completion.

3
00:00:13.247 --> 00:00:20.204
Integration tests can cover uploads, archive processing, subprocess
handoffs, report generation, and background jobs.

4
00:00:20.204 --> 00:00:28.299
Failure tests are especially important because exceptions, timeouts, and
cancellation are where temporary artifacts are often left behind.

5
00:00:28.299 --> 00:00:30.842
Code review should ask practical questions.

6
00:00:30.842 --> 00:00:33.986
Who can influence the temporary file name or directory?

7
00:00:33.986 --> 00:00:35.190
What data is written?

8
00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:36.261
Who can read it?

9
00:00:36.261 --> 00:00:38.000
What permissions are applied?

10
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.813
Does the workflow handle symbolic links or path resolution safely?

11
00:00:41.813 --> 00:00:43.620
Does cleanup run after failure?

12
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.229
Are subprocesses given only necessary paths?

13
00:00:46.229 --> 00:00:47.299
Are logs safe?

14
00:00:47.299 --> 00:00:55.127
These questions keep review focused on the security boundary rather than
treating temporary files as incidental implementation detail.

15
00:00:55.127 --> 00:00:59.877
Logging and monitoring should support operations without leaking sensitive
data.

16
00:00:59.877 --> 00:01:07.370
Logs may record a safe event name, job identifier, storage area, file
category, cleanup status, error category, and correlation ID.

17
00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:13.792
They should avoid private file contents, secrets, tokens, full sensitive
paths, and unnecessary personal data.

18
00:01:13.792 --> 00:01:23.025
Monitoring can watch for disk usage growth, failed cleanup, repeated
processing failures, unexpected temporary directory changes, or unusual
access patterns.

19
00:01:23.025 --> 00:01:35.469
If temporary files expose sensitive data, incident response should assess
what was exposed, where it was stored, who could access it, and whether it
was copied into logs, backups, support bundles, or downstream systems.

20
00:01:35.469 --> 00:01:44.768
Rotate or revoke exposed secrets when needed, delete exposed files where
feasible, preserve necessary evidence, fix the storage design, and add
regression tests.

21
00:01:44.768 --> 00:01:50.120
Temporary file safety improves when incidents lead to better defaults and
cleaner workflows.
